  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 255 AM EDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\n- Dry through first half of the weekend\n\nModels remain persistent on a large upper level low over \nnorth eastern Canada. The decent gradient will \nkeep cooler, drier northwest flow through the region through the\nrest of today into Friday.  \n The clear skies and northwesterly flow could allow for patchy\nfrost along and north of the US 10 corridor. \n A ridge of high pressure will build over the central plains and \nupper midwest through Saturday. The warm air advection will \ncontinue through Saturday with 850mb temps upwards of +17C. This \ncould correlate to temps near 80 across Southern Michigan, which \ncould be 15 to 20 degrees above normal. \n\n- Chance of rain Sunday  \n\nThe warm air advection will continue through Sunday with the\nremnants of the tropical system will trek through the Ohio river\nvalley and overspread the state. Latest track keeps best moisture\nsoutheast of the area. However, Area coinciding with I 69 and to\nthe Southeast could still see showers Sunday. \n\n- Warm with Rain Monday into Tuesday then cooler behind front\n\nAs previous forecasts noted, a strong cold front should swing \nthrough. Latest trends have the positively tilted trough extending\nfrom an upper level low moving through the area Tuesday. This \ncould allow for more showers. It will also drop temperatures from \n10 to 15 degrees above normal on Monday to near normal \ntemperatures and falling Tuesday onward.",
